*Your bone marrow knows you're diabetic before your macrophages do. 🩸*

Day 1/90 - I re-analyzed a PUBLIC dataset GSE54154 (GEO, 2014 - bone marrow progenitors from db/db mice) entirely in Python with new question.

The assumption in textbooks: Diabetes = angry M1 macrophages. Tnf high, Il1b high.

What the public data actually says after I merged 23,360 genes:

Tnf: 0.11 log2FC
Il1b: -0.08
Nos2: -0.35

Barely a whisper. Not a shout.

1979 genes ARE up in diabetic progenitors - but they are Ly6g6f, Tmem150c... progenitor identity genes, not warrior M1 genes.

Understanding from this:

Hyperglycemia doesn't create a warrior in the bone marrow. It creates a _primed recruit_.

The recruit leaves marrow, enters tissue, meets high glucose + second hit - THEN it becomes full M1.

That difference - primed vs warrior - is only visible when you re-question public datasets with M1 panel.

Data source: Public GEO GSE54154 (thank you to original authors)
Re-analysis code + 1979 gene list: github.com/raasenthamil/GSE54154_analysis

